# [M5.0] D27 axis 7 -- caseless 1:n NON-matching (extract Sec 4.1). 11 blocks,
# 2 cases each -> 22 future cases: pcrec's fold is SIMPLE (1:1) like PCRE2's,
# so none of these 11 historically-tempting one-to-many candidates may match
# under `flags i`. Case 1 is a vacuity-guarding positive control (the pattern
# still matches its own literal text); case 2 is the actual 0-of-11 assertion.
# Oracle: libpcre2, PCRE2_UTF|PCRE2_CASELESS.
#
# PROMOTED (lane utfprom, [M5.0] stage 2 close), pinned to today's real
# behaviour per the same stage-4 rule axis06 states in full: all 11 blocks
# compile under `-e utf8 flags i` (the `flags i` line, missing as authored,
# added at promotion) and agree with the oracle on EVERY cell, 0 gaps.
# Unlike axis06, this is NOT a coincidence of today's ASCII-only fold being
# too weak to test the claim: pcrec's ascii-fold trivially can't unify a
# non-ASCII pair, but PCRE2's REAL fold agrees these 11 candidates don't
# unify either (that is the whole point of the "1:n non-matching" design --
# see the extract citation above), so this axis is expected to stay 11/0
# once stage 4 lands too, for its own (checked) reason rather than by
# construction of today's narrower fold.

# axis7 block 1ton-szlig-SS | pattern literal: 'ß' vs partner 'SS'
#   [vacuity: matches itself] m "\xc3\x9f" 0 2   # oracle=PCRE2_UTF|PCRE2_CASELESS
#   [1:n partner, must NOT match] n "SS"   # oracle=PCRE2_UTF|PCRE2_CASELESS
# pcre2-only
pattern ß
encoding utf8
flags i
m "\xc3\x9f" 0 2
n "SS"

# axis7 block 1ton-szlig-ss | pattern literal: 'ß' vs partner 'ss'
#   [vacuity: matches itself] m "\xc3\x9f" 0 2   # oracle=PCRE2_UTF|PCRE2_CASELESS
#   [1:n partner, must NOT match] n "ss"   # oracle=PCRE2_UTF|PCRE2_CASELESS
# pcre2-only
pattern ß
encoding utf8
flags i
m "\xc3\x9f" 0 2
n "ss"

